1
00:00:14,533 --> 00:00:17,133
[engine starts, revs]

2
00:00:26,200 --> 00:00:30,566
[Jerry] This is a 1985 Ferrari 308 GTB.

3
00:00:30,633 --> 00:00:32,600
B for Berlinetta.

4
00:00:33,833 --> 00:00:36,433
QV for Quattrovalvole.

5
00:00:39,900 --> 00:00:43,866
Seven hundred and forty-eight made,
56 for the US.

6
00:00:43,933 --> 00:00:46,166
Three in white.

7
00:00:46,800 --> 00:00:49,800
It has a sharky nose. 
It has a swoopy tail.

8
00:00:51,666 --> 00:00:53,866
It has a mid-engine, three-liter,

9
00:00:53,933 --> 00:00:57,733
240 horsepower,
four valve per cylinder V8.

10
00:00:57,800 --> 00:00:59,166
[engine revving]

11
00:00:59,266 --> 00:01:01,766
Chicks dig it. Dudes want it.

12
00:01:01,833 --> 00:01:04,833
Ferraris are great cars.
They're fun to drive.

13
00:01:04,899 --> 00:01:06,366
And they make people look.

14
00:01:06,433 --> 00:01:09,399
And if you're a young guy
and things are breaking your way,

15
00:01:09,466 --> 00:01:10,766
that's what you need.

16
00:01:10,833 --> 00:01:12,133
It's a really nice car.

17
00:01:12,200 --> 00:01:16,066
Which is why this is the perfect car
for my very special guest this week:

18
00:01:16,576 --> 00:01:18,178
Mr. Trevor Noah.

19
00:01:18,266 --> 00:01:21,133
Trevor Noah is a stand-up comedian
from South Africa,

20
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:25,133
who is the new host of The Daily Show
on Comedy Central.

21
00:01:25,333 --> 00:01:29,000
He has a interesting,
complicated ethnic background

22
00:01:29,066 --> 00:01:32,399
including many colors,
cultures, and languages.

23
00:01:32,466 --> 00:01:33,933
None of that matters.

24
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:36,199
To me, he's just a funny guy.

25
00:01:36,996 --> 00:01:38,431
[phone ringing]

26
00:01:38,500 --> 00:01:41,133
-[Trevor] It's Trevor. Hello.
-[Jerry] Trevor Noah? Jerry Seinfeld.

27
00:01:41,201 --> 00:01:43,536
-[Trevor] Hey, Jerry Seinfeld.
-[Jerry] You're a young guy,

28
00:01:43,603 --> 00:01:46,539
you're hitting it big,
you need to show off a little.

29
00:01:46,633 --> 00:01:49,433
Your job is to give the world
a little thrill.

30
00:01:49,600 --> 00:01:53,199
Just drive, live it up, do your [bleep],
and the hell with it.

31
00:01:53,266 --> 00:01:54,500
[Jerry chuckles]

32
00:01:57,300 --> 00:01:58,500
I'm Jerry Seinfeld, 

33
00:01:58,566 --> 00:02:02,000
and this is
Comedians in Cars Getting Coffee.

34
00:02:05,100 --> 00:02:06,399
Oh, wow. This is nice.

35
00:02:08,361 --> 00:02:10,396
-[Trevor] I love cars.
-[Jerry] I didn't know that.

36
00:02:10,463 --> 00:02:12,665
Oh, my God.
You're the perfect guest for the show.

37
00:02:16,700 --> 00:02:17,966
Wow.

38
00:02:18,033 --> 00:02:20,400
-Not bad.
-That's really nice.

39
00:02:20,466 --> 00:02:23,666
We're like a detective team.
We've got that look to us.

40
00:02:23,733 --> 00:02:27,299
Yeah, that's true.
This is definitely a '70s cop show.

41
00:02:27,433 --> 00:02:30,133
-Yeah, look at us, you know?
-[Jerry] How about Noah and the Man?

42
00:02:30,366 --> 00:02:31,433
[Jerry chuckles]

43
00:02:31,651 --> 00:02:35,021
When I first came to New York,
I went gig-crazy. I didn't...

44
00:02:35,799 --> 00:02:37,900
So, I was doing a show
at the Bleecker Street Theater,

45
00:02:37,966 --> 00:02:41,799
and then after that, I would go
and I'd do six or seven shows.

46
00:02:41,866 --> 00:02:45,299
I'd do an hour and half, and then I'd go
and do six or seven spots,

47
00:02:45,400 --> 00:02:48,333
Thursday to Saturday.
Then Sundays, I'd fly to London.

48
00:02:48,400 --> 00:02:50,600
And then I'd do a show
at a theater there for three days,

49
00:02:50,666 --> 00:02:52,400
then fly back to New York
and do the same.

50
00:02:52,466 --> 00:02:53,833
So I was doing this for a month.

51
00:02:53,900 --> 00:02:57,000
Where did you get that
incredible work ethic?

52
00:02:57,966 --> 00:02:59,200
From being poor.

53
00:02:59,279 --> 00:03:01,314
[both laugh]

54
00:03:03,349 --> 00:03:06,819
[Trevor] That's the weird thing about
trying to explain being a comedian.

55
00:03:06,900 --> 00:03:10,333
Everything has a funny...
Like, everything, literally everything...

56
00:03:10,399 --> 00:03:12,399
-Literally everything...
-Has a funny side.

57
00:03:12,466 --> 00:03:15,433
Like, Nelson Mandela was extremely funny.

58
00:03:15,500 --> 00:03:18,899
But nobody laughed at his jokes,
because it was Nelson Mandela.

59
00:03:18,965 --> 00:03:22,135
They were releasing the Krugerrands
with his face on them.

60
00:03:22,233 --> 00:03:25,866
And so at the press conference,
one of the BBC journalists said,

61
00:03:25,933 --> 00:03:28,633
"Oh, Mr. Mandela, how does it feel
to go from being labeled

62
00:03:28,733 --> 00:03:30,166
as a terrorist in your country

63
00:03:30,233 --> 00:03:34,566
to now having your face
on the currency of that same country?"

64
00:03:34,633 --> 00:03:37,100
So he looked at the journalist and said,

65
00:03:37,166 --> 00:03:39,166
"That's how you know you've made it,

66
00:03:39,533 --> 00:03:41,500
when you have your own money.

67
00:03:41,733 --> 00:03:44,233
No more socializing with poor people."

68
00:03:46,833 --> 00:03:49,933
And I think I heard you say that
your mother is half-Jewish. Is that true?

69
00:03:50,033 --> 00:03:51,700
I think through her grandmother,

70
00:03:51,766 --> 00:03:53,899
because we're mixed in the family
all the way down.

71
00:03:53,966 --> 00:03:55,766
So she'll send me e-mails every day,

72
00:03:55,833 --> 00:03:58,233
and then some of them will be half Hebrew,

73
00:03:58,299 --> 00:04:00,200
and then I don't understand...

74
00:04:01,366 --> 00:04:03,366
like, what she's saying.

75
00:04:03,633 --> 00:04:05,066
[Jerry] E-mails every day...

76
00:04:05,633 --> 00:04:07,233
that sounds Jewish.

77
00:04:11,566 --> 00:04:15,166
The first time in my life I realized
how powerful comedy could be,

78
00:04:15,233 --> 00:04:20,066
was we were in Johannesburg
during a parade/demonstration

79
00:04:20,133 --> 00:04:22,500
and there were policemen on horses.

80
00:04:22,566 --> 00:04:25,300
Everyone was petrified of policemen
on horses because they would beat you.

81
00:04:25,365 --> 00:04:28,101
Everyone just knew this.
And as we're walking through the crowd,

82
00:04:28,166 --> 00:04:29,900
my grandfather's walking with me,

83
00:04:29,966 --> 00:04:32,099
the policeman comes up to us
and he shouts,

84
00:04:32,200 --> 00:04:34,033
"Hey! Get out of the bloody way."

85
00:04:34,099 --> 00:04:37,133
The previous weekend on the news,

86
00:04:37,333 --> 00:04:40,133
the president had been
at a big horse-racing event

87
00:04:40,200 --> 00:04:42,233
and the president had kissed
the winning horse.

88
00:04:42,300 --> 00:04:44,500
It was on the front page,
him kissing the horse.

89
00:04:44,566 --> 00:04:46,000
And my grandfather says,

90
00:04:46,066 --> 00:04:49,000
[imitating grandfather] "Master,
I just need to ask you one question.

91
00:04:49,066 --> 00:04:54,266
"Why is it that your president
can kiss a horse,

92
00:04:55,066 --> 00:04:57,333
but he won't kiss my sister?"

93
00:04:58,400 --> 00:04:59,833
He said, "I don't know why not."

94
00:04:59,900 --> 00:05:04,566
And my grandfather looks at him and says,
"Because you haven't seen my sister."

95
00:05:05,833 --> 00:05:07,466
The guy put his baton down,

96
00:05:07,533 --> 00:05:09,933
he started laughing.
My grandfather laughed.

97
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:14,700
I'd never in my life seen a black man
and a white man laughing, like, together.

98
00:05:14,747 --> 00:05:17,083
Talk-- I'd never ever seen this
in my life.

99
00:05:22,366 --> 00:05:25,066
-[Trevor] Are we in Brooklyn?
-Yeah, we're in Brooklyn, in Dumbo,

100
00:05:25,133 --> 00:05:26,966
which is a neighborhood in Brooklyn

101
00:05:27,033 --> 00:05:30,233
that stands for
"Down Under Manhattan Bridge."

102
00:05:30,300 --> 00:05:32,300
And then they added "Overpass,"

103
00:05:32,366 --> 00:05:34,800
because nobody wants to live in "Dumb."

104
00:05:34,866 --> 00:05:37,333
[indistinct conversation]

105
00:05:40,006 --> 00:05:43,409
Every New Yorker I meet,
they want nothing to do with cars.

106
00:05:43,500 --> 00:05:46,066
New Yorkers are like,
"I don't even have a driver's license."

107
00:05:46,133 --> 00:05:49,500
If New York is the human body,
cars are germs.

108
00:05:51,733 --> 00:05:54,733
That the antibodies
just seek to rid itself

109
00:05:54,800 --> 00:05:58,033
through potholes, rust, and cost.

110
00:05:58,099 --> 00:06:00,866
Get these items out of here.

111
00:06:08,433 --> 00:06:10,099
-How you doing?
-Hey, how are you?

112
00:06:10,166 --> 00:06:12,000
-Coffee?
-That's why we're here.

113
00:06:17,766 --> 00:06:19,733
[Trevor] You really have to
come to South Africa.

114
00:06:19,799 --> 00:06:22,233
Okay. Could we do something
there together?

115
00:06:23,900 --> 00:06:25,133
How could I say no to that?

116
00:06:25,566 --> 00:06:29,133
You have the humility of a colonizer,
that's what you have.

117
00:06:29,933 --> 00:06:31,466
It's like you're holding the gun

118
00:06:31,533 --> 00:06:33,900
and you're saying to me,
as the native on the land,

119
00:06:33,966 --> 00:06:35,566
"Do you think we could share this land?"

120
00:06:36,099 --> 00:06:39,133
-Like I have an option.
-Okay, you got me. I'm doing it.

121
00:06:39,333 --> 00:06:41,733
And it's great, for the family as well.
They can go on safari.

122
00:06:41,799 --> 00:06:43,200
They can, you know, it's like...

123
00:06:43,266 --> 00:06:45,133
I know. My wife's dying to go.

124
00:06:46,400 --> 00:06:47,900
I don't wanna go on safari.

125
00:06:47,966 --> 00:06:51,966
So you see the elephants
and the lions in the wild. I just think,

126
00:06:52,033 --> 00:06:53,633
"So these elephants I'm gonna see,

127
00:06:53,700 --> 00:06:57,033
how similar are they to
the other elephants that I've seen?

128
00:06:59,133 --> 00:07:02,166
They're exactly the same.
Yeah, they're exactly the same."

129
00:07:02,233 --> 00:07:05,566
"But you're seeing them in the wild."
"Yeah, but it's the same elephant."

130
00:07:05,799 --> 00:07:08,533
I'll tell you the thing I'm most
excited about on a safari.

131
00:07:08,700 --> 00:07:09,866
The jeep.

132
00:07:11,964 --> 00:07:14,200
That would be the thing
that would interest me the most.

133
00:07:14,266 --> 00:07:15,733
[both laughing]

134
00:07:23,366 --> 00:07:26,366
South Africa's similar to America.
Like, heckling is not allowed.

135
00:07:26,433 --> 00:07:29,533
In the UK,
it's a very real part of comedy.

136
00:07:29,582 --> 00:07:30,616
I went there.

137
00:07:30,917 --> 00:07:33,719
I didn't even say a joke. Just,
"Hi, I'm Trevor Noah from South Africa."

138
00:07:33,799 --> 00:07:37,000
And then some guy was like,
"Boo, get off the stage!"

139
00:07:37,056 --> 00:07:38,491
And I panicked. I didn't know...

140
00:07:38,558 --> 00:07:40,026
-Yeah.
-I didn't know what to do.

141
00:07:40,099 --> 00:07:42,866
And then I kept quiet.
The audience went quiet.

142
00:07:43,366 --> 00:07:44,366
I said, "Are you done?"

143
00:07:44,433 --> 00:07:47,433
I said, "Forgive me. Where I'm from,
normally when white people come at you,

144
00:07:47,500 --> 00:07:50,000
they come with dogs and tear gas.
Is that all you have?"

145
00:07:50,036 --> 00:07:52,772
-Oh, my God. That's funny.
-And the whole crowd turned on him

146
00:07:52,839 --> 00:07:56,008
because he was heckling the whole night.
So it was... And it was like, "Yeah!"

147
00:07:56,099 --> 00:07:59,233
I would think being South African,
coming from that environment,

148
00:07:59,278 --> 00:08:02,482
your emotional tone is to be tolerant.

149
00:08:02,548 --> 00:08:04,650
Have to. Otherwise,
you'll be angry about everything.

150
00:08:04,733 --> 00:08:06,433
You'll literally be angry
about everything.

151
00:08:06,500 --> 00:08:07,733
Welcome, welcome to America.

152
00:08:07,799 --> 00:08:09,233
[Trevor laughing]

153
00:08:14,060 --> 00:08:17,029
-What's your routine when you wake up?
-I'm really more interested in yours.

154
00:08:17,099 --> 00:08:19,266
My show's at 7:00,
so I wake up at 6:00 p.m.

155
00:08:19,366 --> 00:08:20,466
6:00 p.m.?

156
00:08:20,533 --> 00:08:23,000
Read the news, go into the shower,

157
00:08:23,066 --> 00:08:25,200
get dressed, walk out of my apartment,

158
00:08:25,266 --> 00:08:28,066
I walk into the venue,
walk into the backstage.

159
00:08:28,400 --> 00:08:31,066
The guy will go, "You got
five minutes before you're on."

160
00:08:31,133 --> 00:08:32,900
Then he gives me the mic, and I walk on.

161
00:08:32,966 --> 00:08:35,100
That would be from 7:00 till 8:30.

162
00:08:35,166 --> 00:08:36,633
-At night?
-Yes.

163
00:08:36,699 --> 00:08:39,799
And then I'd leave through the back,
then I'll go have breakfast.

164
00:08:39,900 --> 00:08:42,500
So I find a place, have breakfast,

165
00:08:42,766 --> 00:08:44,833
and then after that I head to
the Comedy Cellar,

166
00:08:44,900 --> 00:08:47,699
do a few spots, then I go to another club,
do a few spots.

167
00:08:47,766 --> 00:08:49,600
Then come back to the Cellar,
then have dinner.

168
00:08:49,666 --> 00:08:51,133
Then I do maybe one more spot.

169
00:08:51,199 --> 00:08:53,866
Chat with comedians,
talk about anything until 3:00 a.m.

170
00:08:53,933 --> 00:08:56,233
Go to the grocery store, do my shopping.

171
00:08:56,300 --> 00:09:00,199
Head back home, and then I'll sleep
at, like, maybe 10:00, 11:00 a.m.

172
00:09:00,766 --> 00:09:03,566
You know what? You're gonna do just fine.

173
00:09:03,633 --> 00:09:05,800
[both chuckling]

174
00:09:07,166 --> 00:09:08,966
You are a purebred.

175
00:09:09,033 --> 00:09:11,233
Despite your mixed ethnic heritage,

176
00:09:11,300 --> 00:09:14,466
your comedic heritage is very purebred.

177
00:09:18,600 --> 00:09:21,133
Just take the highest paid
athletes in the world.

178
00:09:21,199 --> 00:09:25,300
Let's say the top one guy on every team.

179
00:09:25,366 --> 00:09:28,466
American football,
your football, whatever.

180
00:09:28,534 --> 00:09:32,038
It's all the sports, okay?
What made you laugh about 'your football'?

181
00:09:32,133 --> 00:09:33,800
That I'm trying to include you?

182
00:09:33,866 --> 00:09:36,100
No, that you're excluding me.

183
00:09:38,611 --> 00:09:40,079
-The Super Bowl.
-Yeah.

184
00:09:40,146 --> 00:09:42,682
The final, the biggest sporting event
of football

185
00:09:42,748 --> 00:09:45,084
had 100 million viewers watching.

186
00:09:45,233 --> 00:09:47,600
A random match during the season,

187
00:09:47,666 --> 00:09:50,166
not post-season,
not any final, no cup, nothing.

188
00:09:50,266 --> 00:09:51,600
-Yeah.
-A match between Barcelona

189
00:09:51,666 --> 00:09:54,466
and Real Madrid had 400 million viewers.

190
00:09:54,900 --> 00:09:56,666
Clearly, that is the football.

191
00:09:59,666 --> 00:10:03,133
By the rules of democracy,
and just numbers alone,

192
00:10:03,199 --> 00:10:05,000
-that is the football.
-You're right.

193
00:10:05,199 --> 00:10:07,733
-I like the World Cup.
-A lot of Americans like the World Cup.

194
00:10:07,800 --> 00:10:10,433
They like the instantness
or the knockoutness of it, I think.

195
00:10:10,600 --> 00:10:14,000
Yeah, and those look like the Germans.

196
00:10:14,766 --> 00:10:18,300
You know? Those look like
the Mexicans. I get it.

197
00:10:21,066 --> 00:10:22,566
And now, the Mexican team.

198
00:10:22,699 --> 00:10:25,266
They come running out,
"Yeah, that is the Mexican team.

199
00:10:25,333 --> 00:10:27,133
They look so Mexican."

200
00:10:27,199 --> 00:10:29,433
If you just cut that piece out

201
00:10:29,666 --> 00:10:32,000
and the person
who doesn't know Jerry Seinfeld,

202
00:10:32,100 --> 00:10:34,866
doesn't know any part of the--
And you just snip that out, and you go,

203
00:10:34,933 --> 00:10:36,366
"Yeah, that's what I like.

204
00:10:36,433 --> 00:10:38,766
I like the people to look
like where they're from."

205
00:10:38,833 --> 00:10:42,400
That's right.
Americans like cartoons. Crystal clear.

206
00:10:42,466 --> 00:10:43,466
I like that.

207
00:10:48,333 --> 00:10:49,866
I feel like you've taught me
so much already.

208
00:10:49,933 --> 00:10:51,766
You don't need to know anything.

209
00:10:51,833 --> 00:10:53,333
Everything you need to know,

210
00:10:53,400 --> 00:10:55,566
you'll figure it out
when you need to know it.

211
00:10:55,633 --> 00:10:59,533
Even if you miscalculate
and make the wrong decision,

212
00:10:59,600 --> 00:11:01,533
-you needed to know that.
-Yes.

213
00:11:01,600 --> 00:11:06,766
I always say that pain is knowledge
rushing in to fill a gap.

214
00:11:07,066 --> 00:11:10,500
When you stub your toe
on the foot of the bed,

215
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:12,666
that was a gap in knowledge.

216
00:11:12,766 --> 00:11:15,300
[both chuckling]

217
00:11:16,366 --> 00:11:19,699
And the pain is a lot of information
really quick.

218
00:11:19,766 --> 00:11:21,633
-That's what pain is.
-That's very funny.

219
00:11:28,633 --> 00:11:30,966
-Are you on Twitter at all? Okay.
-Yeah.

220
00:11:31,033 --> 00:11:34,966
I'm not of the generation
that got famous from their social media.

221
00:11:35,033 --> 00:11:37,733
In fact, I use it
as a way to just talk to fans.

222
00:11:37,800 --> 00:11:40,833
-In your underwear on your couch.
-[chuckles] Basically.

223
00:11:40,900 --> 00:11:42,966
Doesn't it seem like we're striving

224
00:11:43,033 --> 00:11:46,933
to take the entire life experience
and have it in our underwear?

225
00:11:48,666 --> 00:11:51,166
You can socialize, do work,

226
00:11:51,500 --> 00:11:54,833
get entertained, and get information
all in your underwear.

227
00:11:54,900 --> 00:11:57,633
People are now able to protest
in their underwear.

228
00:11:59,833 --> 00:12:03,133
And that almost defies
what protesting should be about.

229
00:12:03,199 --> 00:12:05,733
The whole point of a protest
is to get out of bed,

230
00:12:05,800 --> 00:12:07,800
put clothes on,
walk out into the cold, and say,

231
00:12:07,866 --> 00:12:09,966
"I stand for this. I march for this."

232
00:12:10,166 --> 00:12:12,966
But now, you don't really
have to have that conviction.

233
00:12:13,033 --> 00:12:14,666
-No.
-'Cause you're on the couch

234
00:12:14,733 --> 00:12:15,733
in your underwear.

235
00:12:15,800 --> 00:12:17,933
Going, "You know?
I don't like it, either."

236
00:12:18,066 --> 00:12:20,199
-In this ten-minute time frame.
-Exactly.

237
00:12:20,266 --> 00:12:23,266
Punch in a few characters and you go,
"Yeah, yeah, I fought for the cause."

238
00:12:23,333 --> 00:12:26,233
No, you didn't. It's the protesters
outside the Chinese thing.

239
00:12:26,300 --> 00:12:29,500
-Of course.
-But there's no anti-protesters,

240
00:12:29,566 --> 00:12:34,166
because people are like "Eh, I like China.
I like iPhones. So I'm not gonna..."

241
00:12:34,233 --> 00:12:37,599
That's almost as bad as,
"Those look like Germans."

242
00:12:38,566 --> 00:12:40,033
That's almost... Maybe worse.

243
00:12:40,099 --> 00:12:42,966
-"I like iPhones, so I like China."
-How is that worse?

244
00:12:43,028 --> 00:12:47,299
Because you've reduced this entire culture
that's billions of years old

245
00:12:47,366 --> 00:12:50,166
and has some significant
human rights issues

246
00:12:50,366 --> 00:12:52,099
to your phone that you like.

247
00:12:52,166 --> 00:12:54,533
So anything else they do is okay with you.

248
00:12:59,411 --> 00:13:01,280
-No. This is mine. You can't.
-Can I pay for it?

249
00:13:01,366 --> 00:13:02,966
-Can I tell you why?
-I don't care why.

250
00:13:03,033 --> 00:13:04,733
Wait a minute, I forgot my wallet.

251
00:13:04,800 --> 00:13:07,466
Oh, that's perfect.
Please, let me. Please.

252
00:13:09,599 --> 00:13:12,266
[indistinct conversation]

253
00:13:12,733 --> 00:13:14,266
Look at this craziness.

254
00:13:14,333 --> 00:13:17,033
They got a merry-go-round
encased in glass.

255
00:13:17,099 --> 00:13:19,033
They need to fill this thing with water.

256
00:13:19,099 --> 00:13:22,000
And then you ride the merry-go-round
with scuba gear.

257
00:13:22,066 --> 00:13:23,333
That would be fun.

258
00:13:24,370 --> 00:13:26,305
[indistinct conversation]

259
00:13:26,438 --> 00:13:30,009
[Jerry] But look at what you get
here in Brooklyn. You get that view.

260
00:13:30,099 --> 00:13:32,733
Don't you think London
has a similar thing?

261
00:13:32,800 --> 00:13:35,466
-All those layers on top of each other?
-Yes.

262
00:13:35,533 --> 00:13:38,366
You get one day a year like this
in London. One.

263
00:13:38,433 --> 00:13:41,400
Yes. Just one. [chuckles]

264
00:13:41,466 --> 00:13:43,500
And yet, there's a faint slither of hope,

265
00:13:43,800 --> 00:13:47,199
because in every pharmacy,
you find them selling sunscreen.

266
00:13:47,666 --> 00:13:49,533
It's "SPF hopeful."

267
00:13:52,733 --> 00:13:54,099
[Jerry] This is Queens.

268
00:13:54,433 --> 00:13:56,366
-Is Flushing in Queens?
-Yes.

269
00:13:56,433 --> 00:13:58,533
Okay. I know that from The Nanny.

270
00:13:58,599 --> 00:13:59,633
Right.

271
00:14:02,500 --> 00:14:04,000
[Jerry] So, in South Africa,

272
00:14:04,043 --> 00:14:06,378
comedy was illegal 21 years ago.

273
00:14:06,445 --> 00:14:07,913
-Yes.
-What are you talking about?

274
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:12,033
It was illegal to stand up on a stage
and tell a joke?

275
00:14:12,099 --> 00:14:15,166
Free speech was against the law,
so let's start there.

276
00:14:15,533 --> 00:14:19,800
So, if you were to get up and
say anything that could be construed as

277
00:14:20,133 --> 00:14:23,233
saying something about
what's happening around you,

278
00:14:23,599 --> 00:14:26,800
and this was open to interpretation
by the government or the police,

279
00:14:26,866 --> 00:14:30,233
you could then be locked up for inciting,
you know, the people.

280
00:14:30,300 --> 00:14:32,566
They believe that what
you were doing was conspiring

281
00:14:32,633 --> 00:14:34,466
-to overthrow the government.
-Wow.

282
00:14:34,533 --> 00:14:37,233
You know, it's like watching
a National Geographic documentary

283
00:14:37,276 --> 00:14:40,279
where they show the plants growing.
When they speed it up in time-lapse.

284
00:14:40,366 --> 00:14:42,099
-Time-lapse.
-That's how we've had comedy.

285
00:14:42,166 --> 00:14:44,533
[Jerry] So when did it
become a viable thing?

286
00:14:44,599 --> 00:14:45,966
[Trevor] Oh, after 1994.

287
00:14:46,099 --> 00:14:49,099
It now became, like,
it is no longer illegal to be black.

288
00:14:49,166 --> 00:14:52,099
-So you can now do things.
-Wow.

289
00:14:52,199 --> 00:14:54,533
So it's like, start by sitting
on the benches.

290
00:14:54,900 --> 00:14:56,466
You know, start by riding in the bus.

291
00:14:56,599 --> 00:14:59,333
Start by walking through the city,
at whatever time.

292
00:14:59,766 --> 00:15:01,800
Then slowly, you go,
"What else can we do?"

293
00:15:01,866 --> 00:15:04,366
You can sing,
you can do shows, you can act.

294
00:15:04,433 --> 00:15:06,599
Can we tell jokes?
Yeah, you can tell jokes.

295
00:15:06,666 --> 00:15:09,699
You'd have guys coming in
from the UK, from the US, going,

296
00:15:09,766 --> 00:15:11,833
"I've been a comedian for 25 years."

297
00:15:11,900 --> 00:15:15,199
"You're telling us you've done a thing
longer than we knew it existed."

298
00:15:16,266 --> 00:15:18,133
[Jerry]
I've been doing comedy a long time.

299
00:15:18,199 --> 00:15:20,099
Yeah, I started when I was 21.

300
00:15:24,233 --> 00:15:26,266
-You're 61 years old?
-Mmm-hmm.

301
00:15:28,566 --> 00:15:30,033
You've aged very well.

302
00:15:30,233 --> 00:15:33,533
Well, I haven't worked my entire life.

303
00:15:36,766 --> 00:15:40,833
Let's go back to your mother and father
getting married during apartheid.

304
00:15:40,900 --> 00:15:43,566
You couldn't get married.
That's the point of apartheid, Jerry.

305
00:15:43,633 --> 00:15:48,300
It is illegal for black and white people
to fraternize with one another.

306
00:15:48,366 --> 00:15:49,766
Couldn't be in a relationship.

307
00:15:49,833 --> 00:15:51,800
My mom would get arrested
when this would happen.

308
00:15:51,866 --> 00:15:52,966
She'd have to pay a fine,

309
00:15:53,033 --> 00:15:55,699
which was a very large amount
of money back then.

310
00:15:55,833 --> 00:15:57,666
-Really?
-Yeah, and you'd pay the fine.

311
00:15:57,733 --> 00:15:59,000
She'd go to jail for a weekend.

312
00:15:59,066 --> 00:16:01,900
She'd pay the fine,
and then she'd do it again.

313
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:05,333
This is apartheid. Apartheid is literally
the perfection of racism.

314
00:16:05,466 --> 00:16:07,733
You separate everybody
into the smallest groups possible.

315
00:16:07,800 --> 00:16:09,766
-The Jews over here.
-Everybody.

316
00:16:09,833 --> 00:16:11,233
Spanish over there.

317
00:16:11,300 --> 00:16:13,300
We don't have Spanish people
in South Africa.

318
00:16:13,366 --> 00:16:14,900
-Um, Russians?
-No Russians.

319
00:16:14,966 --> 00:16:16,833
-Scandinavians?
-No Scandinavians.

320
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:18,966
Kiwis? You got kiwis?

321
00:16:20,166 --> 00:16:21,333
Very few kiwis.

322
00:16:23,433 --> 00:16:26,400
[Jerry] How did your mother and father
spend time together?

323
00:16:26,466 --> 00:16:28,099
We lived in Soweto,

324
00:16:28,166 --> 00:16:31,099
which was the township that all
black people basically lived in.

325
00:16:31,199 --> 00:16:32,199
Right.

326
00:16:32,266 --> 00:16:34,466
As a black person,
you could go into the city to work.

327
00:16:34,533 --> 00:16:36,699
Have your work permit,
you go into the city, you work,

328
00:16:36,766 --> 00:16:38,566
you have to leave
before the sun goes down.

329
00:16:38,766 --> 00:16:40,966
If you're in town, the sun is down,
you get arrested.

330
00:16:41,199 --> 00:16:42,433
That's how it works.

331
00:16:42,500 --> 00:16:45,333
She would act like she was my dad's maid.

332
00:16:45,833 --> 00:16:48,900
And then, that's how
we'd get into his apartment.

333
00:16:48,966 --> 00:16:52,099
She had an outfit in a bag.
And not like sexy French maid.

334
00:16:52,166 --> 00:16:54,266
-Literally, blue collar.
-Right.

335
00:16:54,333 --> 00:16:56,633
You know, overalls, time to work.

336
00:16:56,699 --> 00:16:59,366
What were you in this little play?

337
00:16:59,433 --> 00:17:01,933
She would say that she was babysitting me

338
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:04,466
for a friend of hers
that was my skin color.

339
00:17:04,533 --> 00:17:06,366
So you have white, you have black.

340
00:17:06,433 --> 00:17:10,500
The problem is, every time
people break the rules, this comes out.

341
00:17:10,566 --> 00:17:13,566
But in South Africa, this is not black.
They don't call me black.

342
00:17:13,633 --> 00:17:16,666
They say you're colored. Black is black.
White is white. Colored is colored.

343
00:17:16,733 --> 00:17:19,033
So then what happened was,
people would go,

344
00:17:19,099 --> 00:17:23,000
"How do you have a colored baby?
Who helped you commit this crime?"

345
00:17:23,066 --> 00:17:25,466
So then, my mom would go,
"No, this is not my baby."

346
00:17:25,644 --> 00:17:27,846
-Oh, my God.
-"This is my colored friend's baby.

347
00:17:27,933 --> 00:17:29,600
And then, I'm just looking after him."

348
00:17:29,666 --> 00:17:31,966
-And this is late '80s?
-Yes.

349
00:17:32,399 --> 00:17:35,933
-These people, these people.
-The whole system was absurd.

350
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:37,399
It doesn't make sense.

351
00:17:37,466 --> 00:17:41,000
Racism, all of these things, when you look
at them, they don't make sense.

352
00:17:41,100 --> 00:17:42,733
You know, Hitler... It doesn't make...

353
00:17:42,800 --> 00:17:45,500
When you look now, you go,
"How did that craziness happen?

354
00:17:45,566 --> 00:17:47,833
-It doesn't make sense."
-Right.

355
00:17:47,899 --> 00:17:49,266
There's just a moment in time.

356
00:17:49,333 --> 00:17:53,300
If you find the right balance
between desperation and fear...

357
00:17:53,338 --> 00:17:55,941
-Right. That's correct.
-...you can make people believe anything.

358
00:17:57,466 --> 00:17:58,899
[Jerry] Apartheid ended what year?

359
00:17:58,966 --> 00:18:01,333
The date always throws me
because there was, like,

360
00:18:01,399 --> 00:18:02,933
a soft release, then like a...

361
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:05,466
-Soft opening?
-Yeah. There was literally a...

362
00:18:05,533 --> 00:18:07,433
They did that at
Marvel Adventureland, too.

363
00:18:08,266 --> 00:18:10,500
They don't just open
all the rides before they...

364
00:18:10,566 --> 00:18:12,366
-Start slowly.
-Yeah, they start slow.

365
00:18:13,866 --> 00:18:16,433
You're a comic, you're knocking around.

366
00:18:16,500 --> 00:18:20,866
Let's talk about when you became
the new host of The Daily Show.

367
00:18:20,933 --> 00:18:24,433
First of all, do you know that
they're even thinking of you for this gig?

368
00:18:24,470 --> 00:18:26,638
I was like, who's gonna get the job?
This is crazy.

369
00:18:26,705 --> 00:18:28,807
Everyone at the show was like,
"Who's gonna get the job?"

370
00:18:28,899 --> 00:18:32,533
Jon announces he's leaving,
it throws everybody off.

371
00:18:32,733 --> 00:18:36,300
Because Jon does what no one seems to do.

372
00:18:36,366 --> 00:18:38,300
And that's leave when the time is right.

373
00:18:38,366 --> 00:18:39,700
Why is it time?

374
00:18:39,766 --> 00:18:45,100
Why wouldn't he stay through the election,
which is the juiciest time for that show?

375
00:18:45,433 --> 00:18:49,399
It's a political comedy show,
and a presidential election is coming up.

376
00:18:49,466 --> 00:18:52,633
How is that good timing?
Please. Explain that.

377
00:18:52,698 --> 00:18:54,500
He's got an eight-year-old
and a ten-year-old.

378
00:18:54,566 --> 00:18:56,301
-It's the juiciest time in their lives.
-Come on.

379
00:18:56,366 --> 00:19:01,133
This is the sick parenting culture
that we have in this country now.

380
00:19:04,800 --> 00:19:07,533
-Tell me where you are when you find out?
-I'm in Dubai.

381
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:11,800
I'm literally driving in the middle
of the night and my phone rings.

382
00:19:13,051 --> 00:19:17,556
And my manager says "How would you like
to be the host of The Daily Show?"

383
00:19:20,200 --> 00:19:21,766
So, what did you do physically?

384
00:19:21,833 --> 00:19:25,266
I get out the car and my legs...
I didn't have legs.

385
00:19:25,933 --> 00:19:28,666
You know in those movies
where there's an explosion?

386
00:19:28,733 --> 00:19:31,200
-Yeah.
-But instead of the sound of the explosion

387
00:19:31,266 --> 00:19:32,800
you hear the silence.

388
00:19:32,866 --> 00:19:35,066
The eardrums have all been pierced...

389
00:19:35,133 --> 00:19:36,533
That's literally what happens.

390
00:19:36,575 --> 00:19:39,077
And then, the worst thing is,
you're in Dubai,

391
00:19:39,144 --> 00:19:41,647
which is one of the hardest places
in the world to find a drink,

392
00:19:41,733 --> 00:19:44,166
and then you go, "Let's find a drink."

393
00:19:44,399 --> 00:19:47,800
Let's find a drink in this Muslim,
Middle-Eastern country.

394
00:19:48,100 --> 00:19:51,033
Even if we're getting arrested,
this is worth it, let's get that drink.

395
00:19:51,200 --> 00:19:54,433
-Where is a drink?
-It's very closed-off, very...

396
00:19:54,493 --> 00:19:56,094
It's not secretive,

397
00:19:56,161 --> 00:19:59,565
but they keep the alcohol
away from the public, if that makes sense.

398
00:19:59,631 --> 00:20:02,734
I see. So now we have liquid apartheid.

399
00:20:05,100 --> 00:20:07,433
-You can't get away from it.
-You can't, Jerry.

400
00:20:07,500 --> 00:20:09,133
-One fight at a time.
-Yeah.

401
00:20:10,500 --> 00:20:12,033
Well, congratulations, my friend.

402
00:20:12,100 --> 00:20:14,133
-Thank you very much.
-I'm very happy for you.

403
00:20:15,133 --> 00:20:16,700
Thank you very much, Jerry.

404
00:20:18,050 --> 00:20:20,652
[Jerry] Would you like to go
to the Mets game? Wanna go to see...

405
00:20:20,719 --> 00:20:23,121
-[Trevor] Yeah, I'd love to.
-Fantastic, let's do that.

406
00:20:27,092 --> 00:20:31,230
I feel like people need to know
that Jerry Seinfeld called Trevor Noah

407
00:20:31,300 --> 00:20:32,766
long before The Daily Show said...

408
00:20:32,833 --> 00:20:34,233
-Right.
-..."We want you as the host."

409
00:20:34,333 --> 00:20:37,033
I had asked you to do the show
before any of this happened.

410
00:20:37,233 --> 00:20:39,666
Yes, I hope you make that known
to everybody.

411
00:20:39,733 --> 00:20:41,700
'Cause I feel like
you deserve that credit.

412
00:20:41,766 --> 00:20:43,899
Because now I seem like an obvious choice.

413
00:20:43,966 --> 00:20:44,899
Yeah.

414
00:20:44,966 --> 00:20:47,500
And I feel like you're not
an obvious choice kind of guy.

415
00:20:48,066 --> 00:20:49,399
Because I think, "This guy's cool.

416
00:20:49,466 --> 00:20:52,533
I like his thing.
I wanna get to know him."

